{
  "title": "Proximal Tib-Fib Dislocation",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/knee-and-sports/3014/proximal-tib-fib-dislocation",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Jun 19 2023",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "A proximal tib-fib dislocation is a disruption of the proximal tibia-fibula joint associated with high energy open fractures of the tibia and peroneal nerve injury.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis requires careful assessment of radiographs of the knee and tibia (often missed injury).",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is prompt closed reduction with unstable injuries requiring surgical pinning versus soft tissue reconstruction.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Incidence",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**rare injury**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "even less common as an isolated injury",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Demographics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "most common in 2nd to 4th decades",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mechanism",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**high-energy trauma**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "more common with horseback riding and parachuting",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "fall onto a **flexed and adducted knee**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Associated conditions",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "posterior hip dislocation (flexed knee and hip)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "open tibia-fibula fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "other fractures about the knee and ankle",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Arthrology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3014/images/proximal%20tibiofibular%20joint.jpg",
              "caption": "Shows the anatomy of the proximal tibiofibular joint"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "proximal fibula articulates with a facet of the lateral cortex of the tibia",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "distinct from the articulation of the knee",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "joint is strengthened by anterior and posterior ligaments of the fibular head",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3014/images/proximal%20tibiofibular%20joint%20anterior%20ligament.jpg",
                  "caption": "Show the anterior ligament of the proximal tibiofibular joint"
                },
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3014/images/proximal%20tibiofibular%20joint%20posterior%20ligament.jpg",
                  "caption": "Show the posterior ligament of the proximal tibiofibular joint."
                }
              ],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Nerves",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**common peroneal nerve** lies distal to the proximal tibiofibular joint on the posterolateral aspect of the fibular neck",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3014/images/common%20peroneal%20nerve%20illustration%20anterior%20and%20posterior%20views.jpg",
                  "caption": "Shows the course of the common peroneal nerve"
                },
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3014/images/common%20peroneal%20nerve.jpg",
                  "caption": "Shows the course of the common peroneal nerve in a posterior view of the knee"
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Classification",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Ogden classification",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3014/images/proximal%20tibiofibular%20dislocation%20classification.jpg",
              "caption": "Shows the Ogden classification of proximal tibiofibular joint injuries"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "subluxation and 3 types of dislocation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**anterolateral - most common**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3014/images/proximal%20tibiofibular%20joint%20anterolateral%20dislocation.jpg",
                      "caption": "Show an anterolateral dislocation of the proximal tibiofibular joint"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "posteromedial",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3014/images/proximal%20tibiofibular%20joint%20posteromedial%20dislocation.jpg",
                      "caption": "Show a posteromedial dislocation of the proximal tibiofibular joint"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "superior",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3014/images/proximal%20tibiofibular%20joint%20superior%20dislocation.jpg",
                      "caption": "Show a superior dislocation of the proximal tibiofibular joint with an associated high-energy tibia fracture"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Presentation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Symptoms",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "lateral knee pain",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "symptoms can mimic a lateral meniscal tear",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "instability",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Physical exam",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "tenderness about the fibular head",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**comparison of bilateral knees** with palpation of normal anatomic landmarks and their relative positions can clarify the diagnosis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Imaging",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Radiographs",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "2",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "recommended views",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "AP and lateral of both knees",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3014/images/proximal%20right%20tibiofibular%20joint%20dislocation.jpg",
                      "caption": "Shows an anterolateral dislocation of the right proximal tibiofibular joint"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**comparison views of the contralateral knee are essential**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "CT scan",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3014/images/proximal%20tibiofibular%20joint%20dislocation%20ct.jpg",
              "caption": "Shows an anterolateral dislocation of the right proximal tibiofibular joint"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "clearly identifies the presence or absence of dislocation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Treatment",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Nonoperative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "closed reduction",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "acute dislocations",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "technique",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**flex knee** 80°-110° and **apply pressure over the fibular head opposite to the direction of dislocation**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "post-reduction immobilization in **extension vs. early range of motion (controversial)**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "outcomes",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "commonly successful with minimal disadvantages",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Operative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "surgical soft tissue stabilization vs. open reduction and pinning vs. arthrodesis vs. fibular head resection",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3014/images/proximal%20tibiofibular%20joint%20pinning.jpg",
                  "caption": "Shows a closed reduction and pinning of the proximal tibiofibular joint"
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "chronic dislocation with chronic pain and symptomatic instability",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Complications",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "**Recurrence**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "**Common peroneal nerve injury**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**usually seen with posterior dislocations**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "occurs at higher rate (>35%) with concomitant proximal tibia fracture",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Arthritis**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "rarely occurs and is usually minimally symptomatic",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
